{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00488 - MONDO_16248",
  "verbalization": "Altretamine, a chemotherapy agent, is associated with Familial Ovarian Cancer through its interaction with Cisplatin, which is a known inducer of ovarian cancer. The evidence suggests that Altretamine may have a role in the pathogenesis of Familial Ovarian Cancer, as it is indicated in tumors such as Malignant Dysgerminomatous Germ Cell Tumor Of Ovary, Malignant Sertoli-leydig Cell Tumor Of Ovary, and Ovarian Mucinous Adenocarcinoma, all of which are linked to Cisplatin. This indicates a potential mechanistic link between Altretamine and the development of ovarian cancer, particularly in the context of Cisplatin-induced malignancies.\n\nThe integration of these paths suggests that Altretamine may exert a modulatory effect on tumor progression, possibly through mechanisms that influence the response to Cisplatin. The presence of a consistent pathway involving Cisplatin and the associated tumors implies that Altretamine could be a candidate for repurposing in the treatment of Familial Ovarian Cancer, particularly in cases where Cisplatin is used as a therapeutic agent. The evidence supports the hypothesis that Altretamine may contribute to the pathogenesis of Familial Ovarian Cancer by interacting with the tumor microenvironment or by influencing the efficacy of Cisplatin in treating these malignancies.",
